The "little" ones are nine. The eldest is five weeks short of eleven.
She can read simple English, speak enough English to talk to kids her
own age and younger (and patient adults), understand some French and
German, a word or two of Danish, and slightly more Greek, but no
proficiency in any of those to speak of. That's probably because we do
have English-speaking people around every now and again, but no French
or German or Greek-speaking people, and we have to depend on holidays
(once a year and to a different place every time).

The little ones have a smattering of English and German (and can puzzle
out the Greek alphabet). The youngest is particularly gifted with
German: she has my ear for accents. (Doesn't seem to help with French,
probably because that doesn't have as many familiar words)

As for the classics, we tried to teach them Latin but other things
turned out to be more interesting/important/time-consuming at the time.

I think the critical ages for learning languages are under four, and
between ten and twelve. I wish high school started earlier.

